WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.520
Ooh, off the heel. Not good.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.880
You would get made fun
of so bad in parkour

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.020
if you were doing that.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.560
My name is Lorena Abreu,

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.280
and I am a professional
parkour athlete and stuntwoman.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.100
I started parkour in 2013,

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.410
then got to do some parkour
for Cirque du Soleil.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.730
Today, we're going to be
looking at parkour stunts

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.789
for film and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.410
I love this scene,

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.520
because it's shot kind
of like a parkour video.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.040
You know, mostly wide.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.982
I like just being able to
see the parkour clearly.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.950
So, technique-wise, the
underbar, excellent.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.010
You go feetfirst, you
catch with your arms,

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.830
and you pull the bar
through you, like this.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.290
I don't really see them
doing anything for no reason,

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.200
which tends to happen a lot

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.258
in other films that have
parkour chase scenes.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.280
It looks like the two ninja space guys

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.040
are only doing kong vaults in this scene.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.530
A kong is a vault where you use both hands

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.200
to push down on the wall
that you're vaulting over,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.910
and your feet come through the center.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.140
In real life, if these
guys were actually running

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.870
for their absolute lives,

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.110
I think some of those kong vaults

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.670
would've been safeties instead.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.370
A safety vault is when you put
one hand down on the obstacle

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.300
and one foot down on the obstacle.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.410
The other foot comes through the middle.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.950
So, the reason that one
is the most efficient

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.850
is because you can
actually push off that foot

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.640
that's planted on the wall and get speed.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.470
Fennec is actually doing
mostly safety vaults

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.800
and speed vaults.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.010
A speed vault is a safety vault,

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.502
but without putting your foot down.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.390
Vertical pole swings are one
of my favorite things ever.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:01.120
Their technique for the
vertical pole swing was great.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.650
They led with their arms

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.635
and swung their legs
behind them and around

00:02:05.635 --> 00:02:07.458
to redirect their momentum.

00:02:09.316 --> 00:02:12.545
I would not recommend swan-diving

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:14.350
off of a building chest-first

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.560
unless you were competing
in a belly-flop competition

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.860
and landing in a pool, on some water.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.200
So, what I would do
instead is do a turn vault

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.884
and climb down.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.930
For me, it's a 10 out of 10.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.115
It is 100% legit parkour,

00:02:31.115 --> 00:02:33.520
unless you include the swan dive.

00:02:34.907 --> 00:02:36.391
[grunting]

00:02:37.998 --> 00:02:38.831
Parkour!

00:02:38.831 --> 00:02:40.300
This scene from "The Office"

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.040
is the bane of so many
parkour athletes' existence,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.997
but I love it!

00:02:46.997 --> 00:02:49.470
This is parkour.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.700
Internet sensation of 2004.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.330
And it was in one of the Bond films.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.260
It's pretty impressive.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.260
The goal is to get from point A to point B

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.926
as creatively as possible.

00:02:57.926 --> 00:03:01.500
The clips he showed on
the laptop were legit.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.700
Their guy was doing tic
tacs and 180 wall runs

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.670
and, you know, one of those
old-school 2004 wall flips.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.150
I think this was great
exposure for parkour,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.790
because Jim's definition was legit

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.710
and put the word "parkour" in
a lot of people's vocabulary.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:20.610
And, yeah, the downsides is that

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.470
a lot of people didn't take it seriously.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.870
Nobody likes getting
yelled, "hardcore parkour!"

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.060
in the streets while we're training.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.650
It happens all the time.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:28.687
We go one at a time.

00:03:28.687 --> 00:03:29.980
Here it is, here it is.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:33.530
Truck to refrigerators to dumpster,

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.030
360 spin onto the pallets.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.565
That's actually probably
the most realistic portrayal

00:03:37.565 --> 00:03:39.110
of planning a parkour line

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.150
that I've seen in film or television.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.500
We call sequences of movements

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.997
that we usually film and post
on YouTube or on Instagram,

00:03:46.997 --> 00:03:49.613
a run. We're standing around,

00:03:49.613 --> 00:03:51.247
we see something that we want to do,

00:03:51.247 --> 00:03:52.927
"Oh, I can do this from here to there."

00:03:52.927 --> 00:03:54.810
"OK, now how do I connect it all?"

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.970
Backflip gainer into the trash can.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.905
Dwight: Yeah, gainer!
Michael: Yeah, yeah, yeah!

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.928
A gainer is a backflip
that travels forwards.

00:04:01.928 --> 00:04:04.610
The level of talent has increased

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.290
so dramatically in recent years

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.640
that I actually think there
are now multiple people

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.610
that could probably do a running gainer

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.840
off of that wooden platform
into the recycling bin.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.503
But, yeah, that's just not really a thing.

00:04:20.503 --> 00:04:22.040
I want to rate it 10 out of 10

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.985
for how much I enjoyed it.

00:04:23.985 --> 00:04:25.680
In terms of parkour realism,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.897
I don't know, a 2.

00:04:30.941 --> 00:04:32.320
Doing a descent like that,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.810
going straight from arms

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.170
to catching only with your arms again,

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.890
you would rip your shoulders
out of their sockets

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.850
unless you were unbelievably strong.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.533
They could have made this scene

00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:47.141
10,000 times more realistic

00:04:47.141 --> 00:04:49.270
by doing the actual descent technique

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.360
of having her hit her feet first

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.090
before landing on the lower walls

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.048
with her hands in a cat position.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:00.260
The technique was super
legit in her takeoff.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.130
The pressure she's doing up
on the window, that's legit.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:05.090
You can definitely stay
there in that position.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.540
Her little prep ops to the side

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.610
before jumping this way,
all of that was legit.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.070
The only thing that was
not legit with that move

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.540
was the distance.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.540
You know how hard it is to jump that far

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.408
without coming off of the wall?

00:05:21.408 --> 00:05:22.810
So, she has to jump that hard

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:27.230
and somehow stay skating
on the surface of the wall

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.105
to go that far over there.

00:05:29.105 --> 00:05:30.138
Uh, no.

00:05:30.138 --> 00:05:32.810
I rate this scene from
"Alice in Borderland"

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.310
a 2.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.390
and his run up the beam, 100% authentic.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:48.790
A cat leap in parkour

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.880
is when you jump to the face of a wall,

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.810
so you're either hanging
onto the top of the wall,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.030
which is the most common,

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.520
or you're grabbing onto the sides

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.950
and crimping it, like Sébastien just did.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.600
Sébastien Foucan is one of
the founders of parkour.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.380
He can probably do it even faster.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.190
And just like anything else in parkour,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.110
you always want to go feetfirst,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.750
because feet forwards is the rule.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.920
It's the law, and you can never break it.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.870
Unless you're doing a
dive kong or a dive roll.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.670
You never want to go hands-first,

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.450
There are plenty of guys
that can do that no problem

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.870
and much higher and on
things much skinnier.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.560
I'd imagine that Craig or his stunt double

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.760
The run-up for the jump was excellent.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.020
So, the most important factor

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.630
that goes into doing a jump like this,

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.410
or any jump, is your run-up,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.190
your last three steps,
and your foot placement.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.650
That is the parkour bible.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.960
More experienced athlete
will know to map out

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.810
their last three steps way ahead of time.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.600
I don't think Craig took stutter steps,

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.799
which, maybe he should have.

00:07:02.799 --> 00:07:04.850
It's really, really easy
to mix up your steps

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.500
when you're going into a
running jump in parkour.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.380
You can see the difference
in parkour technique

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.830
between Sébastien and Craig.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.410
Sébastien's character has a more tight,

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.750
compact air form in the
middle of his jumps,

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.620
whereas Craig's body is more extended

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.200
and a little bit less in control.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.290
So, in parkour,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.440
that's why you're going to
see us tuck into a ball.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.150
We're bringing our knees to our chest

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.050
and extending our feet forwards

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.370
to position your feet
correctly for the landing.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:34.350
So, it makes sense that
Daniel Craig's character

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.610
That was a massive drop.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.268
In real life, the reason why

00:07:44.268 --> 00:07:46.893
someone like Mr. Bond here

00:07:46.893 --> 00:07:48.980
would've missed his feet the way he did

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:50.685
would be because he didn't get his feet

00:07:50.685 --> 00:07:52.250
properly in front of him.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.840
So, when you do a drop precision,

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.700
you generally don't tuck
into a ball in the air.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.170
You always want to be aiming your feet

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:01.820
at what you're landing on.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:03.830
So, he undershot it.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:05.120
His chest was too far forward.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.210
beautiful parkour roll.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.870
You can take some gnarly height drops

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:22.200
if you can roll correctly.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.660
We generally go hands-first, at an angle,

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.800
so you don't get too much impact

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.170
in any one part of your body.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:41.592
I don't know if this had to do with

00:08:41.592 --> 00:08:43.925
the weird little, whichaw! split-leg thing

00:08:43.925 --> 00:08:46.492
that he did at the takeoff for no reason.

00:08:46.492 --> 00:08:50.070
But he actually did a perfectly
fine roll when he landed.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.830
It just appears in the shot

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.370
that he had too much momentum

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.082
that sent him flying off.

00:08:55.082 --> 00:08:57.374
That's realistic. That
can definitely happen.

00:08:57.374 --> 00:08:58.880
Based on realism,

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.530
I would have to give it a 9 out of 10.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.560
The techniques and the
physics of everything

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.642
was all fantastic.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.060
Ooh, off the heel. Not good.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.420
You would get made fun
of so bad in parkour

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.660
if you were doing that.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.700
Anytime you're doing anything in parkour,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.290
especially a stride,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.100
which is a jump off of one leg,

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.670
and especially if it's
off something skinny,

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:24.880
you want to perform the movement

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.470
off the ball of your foot,

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:29.303
for safety and power and efficiency.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.100
His arm movements are legit.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.440
Whenever you're jumping at something,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:37.390
you want to send your arms

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:38.820
in the direction of
what you're jumping at.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.353
If you're doing a stride, you'll often do

00:09:41.353 --> 00:09:43.020
opposite arms to legs.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:43.895
That's legit.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.250
You can definitely do that.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:51.540
If you're doing a massive jump,

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:53.190
you don't really want to land

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.648
in a stride on just one leg.

00:09:54.648 --> 00:09:57.280
It's a lot of impact for no reason.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.780
I would rate it, like, an 8 out of 10,

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:02.480
because he's doing real parkour,

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:04.809
just with bad technique.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:14.090
I love how he's just
randomly doing parkour

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:15.740
in random places.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.260
Parkour generally consists
of jumping from things

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.210
to other things and
not just off of things.

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.440
So, the spots where we'd actually train

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.007
to practice parkour is walls

00:10:26.007 --> 00:10:28.920
that are more or less near other walls.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.000
But we can also practice on grass,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.600
to practice tricks, to practice falling,

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.908
which is extremely
important, falling safely.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.678
In terms of the skills

00:10:41.678 --> 00:10:43.670
that he's practicing in this montage,

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:48.400
that is 100% realistic
and expected of someone

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.950
who is just starting to do parkour.

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:51.950
So, when you first learn parkour,

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.040
you are going to learn the jumps.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:57.930
He did a little running jump
stride off of the end of that.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.030
Didn't land with great technique,

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:01.570
but it's fine 'cause he's a beginner.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:03.010
You're going to learn the vaults.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.890
That was a lazy vault over the car.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.674
It's all legit parkour.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:16.000
The sketchiness of doing
a full-speed running jump

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.560
from one level of a parking garage

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.290
down to another level of a parking garage

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.840
when you're a beginner is just, no.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.360
No a todo.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:26.880
He had, like, no run-up.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.520
He took, what, like, five
steps into that giant gap?

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:32.870
And not even trying to sell the landing.

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:35.790
Like, they literally just cut
to him being on another floor.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.950
Without the last scene,

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.630
I would rate this a 9
out of 10 for realism.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:43.021
Big props to Taylor Lautner.

00:11:48.334 --> 00:11:51.880
I don't know if they actually
scaled that entire building,

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.740
but there's no reason to
believe they wouldn't,

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.700
because the Yamakasi absolutely
did things like that.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:04.040
So, the Yamakasi are the
quintessential parkour team

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:07.220
that invented what became parkour

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:08.379
as we know it today.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.360
I can't see much detailing in those shoes.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:17.100
I don't know if that was an advertisement.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.290
You can technically do
parkour in any shoes,

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.930
and you can do it barefoot too.

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:23.100
You should train barefoot, actually,

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.950
frequently, to refine your technique

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.600
and strengthen all the
muscles in your feet.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.190
So, these shoes that I'm wearing,

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:33.560
the version with laces
is actually really good.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.275
That is an ideal midsole

00:12:36.275 --> 00:12:39.267
that will protect you from most impact.

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:42.930
Things like these flips,

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.100
if we're talking parkour,
they're not efficient,

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.740
but they're obviously fun,

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:47.820
and they're stylish.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.740
We tend to use the term freerunning

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.500
to describe anything that's nonefficient

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.800
that's generally used for style,

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.542
whereas we use parkour to describe

00:12:57.542 --> 00:12:59.220
efficient and power movements.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.610
So, I like to look at it like a spectrum,

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:04.210
with parkour on one end and
freerunning on the other.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.340
I, for example, land somewhere over here.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:07.680
Like, I can do some flips,

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.818
but I am mostly out jumping at stuff.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:16.590
So, it looks like they
were using their legs a lot

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.080
in scaling that building.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.660
When it comes to scaling things

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.810
and when it comes to
doing parkour in general,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.550
you want to use your
legs as much as possible,

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:27.450
because they are the
strongest muscles in your body

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.450
and the ones that take
the longest to exhaust.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:33.920
When they were going up
on those concrete things,

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.130
you could see that their
arms were mostly lax

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.821
and they were pushing with
their legs to come up on it.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.760
Taking height drops,
those early speed vaults

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.500
with the arm up here,
super Yamakasi style.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.640
You can see the Yamakasi

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:51.820
all kind of doing their own thing.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:54.250
Everyone's doing something
similar but slightly different.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:55.770
So, in real life,

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.490
what would determine which
moves you would choose

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:00.100
depends on a number of factors.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:01.940
One, what do you want to do?

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:04.320
Because we're generally
doing parkour for fun.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.480
Two, your height could play a role in it.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.740
A jump might be huge for
you if you're my size,

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.532
but it might be small for
you if you're someone else.

00:14:12.532 --> 00:14:15.010
I would have to give it
at least a 12 out of 10,

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:16.527
because these are the Yamakasi

00:14:16.527 --> 00:14:18.018
doing what the Yamakasi does.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.690
A lot of people have asked the question

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:31.025
of whether or not what Jackie
Chan did in these movies

00:14:31.025 --> 00:14:32.450
could be considered parkour,

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.210
because he was technically doing it

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:37.431
before anyone coined the term parkour.

00:14:37.431 --> 00:14:38.760
My answer to the question,

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.850
and what I think is the
objective correct answer anyways,

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:43.410
is yes.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:44.460
I mean, look at him!

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:46.250
Like, that's a running plyo.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:51.150
A plyo is when you do
a jump with both feet

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:52.130
to land with both feet

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.060
and immediately jump again with both feet.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.010
The idea is that you are
absorbing with both feet

00:14:57.010 --> 00:14:58.793
and using that absorption

00:14:58.793 --> 00:15:00.884
as the prep for your next takeoff.

00:15:00.884 --> 00:15:02.520
He's throwing his arms the way you should,

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:05.240
but, again, his chest
is way too far forward

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.315
and he landed face-first into something.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.970
He is jumping onto all
kinds of sketchy stuff here.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.870
Things that are soft and unreliable.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:25.670
In real life, we are not going
to be jumping to fabrics.

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:29.820
There's just so much risk for ankles,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.659
for the thing itself
collapsing, like it did there,

00:15:33.659 --> 00:15:36.200
for it launching you in
a way that's unexpected.

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:38.350
We generally like

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:42.385
reliable hard surfaces that are even.

00:15:42.385 --> 00:15:44.480
A lot of the moves that Jackie Chan does

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.670
in these old movies,

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:48.070
he's famous for his wall runs,

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.250
and he'll do speed vaults and laches,

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.460
which is swinging off a
bar and throwing yourself.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:54.210
Those are things that
Jackie Chan is known for

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.470
that fit into the modern toolbox

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.760
of parkour moves that we know of today.

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.502
For realism, it's a 10 out of 10,

00:16:02.502 --> 00:16:04.109
because it's Jackie Chan.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:10.130
So, this move that David Belle does,

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:13.490
you don't usually see that
very often in real life.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:17.790
Unless there was a gap
that was too big to jump,

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:19.740
so you would need to run on the wall

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:22.480
or off a ledge on the wall
to get to the other side,

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.870
or to get around some bad guys,

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:25.820
like David Belle.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:28.760
But we generally use the term wall run

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:33.390
to describe going up a wall to tac at,

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:34.650
towards the bottom of the wall,

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.084
at an angle with your
foot to shoot you upwards

00:16:37.084 --> 00:16:40.190
to scale a wall that is too
tall to otherwise get up.

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:42.880
A lot of people credit him with
being the father of parkour,

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.540
but based on my understanding
of how things went down,

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.531
he invented parkour

00:16:49.531 --> 00:16:51.997
along with the rest of the Yamakasi.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.970
I love the chase-down-the-stairway scene.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.540
What makes this scene unique,

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.600
compared to a lot of other
parkour scenes from film and TV,

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:06.577
is that David Belle is
doing a lot of parkour

00:17:06.577 --> 00:17:08.920
in a closed and cramped space.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:11.600
He does a few moves for
style points in here

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:12.750
that aren't very efficient,

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.343
like he does a reverse at one point,

00:17:15.343 --> 00:17:16.943
as he's bounding down the stairs.

00:17:16.943 --> 00:17:19.360
But grabbing on to the railings

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:23.076
and generally either doing a speed vault

00:17:23.076 --> 00:17:25.042
or a turn vault

00:17:25.042 --> 00:17:27.433
to bound and plyo down

00:17:27.433 --> 00:17:30.620
is 100% realistic and
exactly what you would do

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:31.862
if you were trying to get down the stairs

00:17:31.862 --> 00:17:33.117
that fast doing parkour.

00:17:34.542 --> 00:17:36.192
Vault to slam the door on the guy.

00:17:36.192 --> 00:17:38.133
How'd he know he was
there? Was he psychic?

00:17:38.133 --> 00:17:39.550
I don't know how he saw
him from the other side

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:40.479
of that door.

00:17:40.479 --> 00:17:42.093
Explosion!

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:50.980
Classic Yamakasi dramatic roof gap.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.710
David Belle has definitely
done roof gaps that big.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.905
He did a very dramatic, kind of flaily,

00:17:56.905 --> 00:18:00.630
classic Yamakasi getting away
from something explosive.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.090
In real life, you would have

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:04.250
a much more controlled air form.

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:07.610
You would take off and, like I said,

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:09.700
bring your knees in the
direction of your chest,

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:12.570
aim your feet at the landing,
and roll upon impact,

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:14.980
just like David Belle
perfectly did in here.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:18.290
So, if I were to rate
this based on realism,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.381
I would rate it

00:18:20.381 --> 00:18:23.956
a 7 or 8 out of 10,

00:18:23.956 --> 00:18:25.330
because I can't help but think

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:27.150
about a little bit more
than just the parkour

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.610
because of his psychic ability

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:31.540
to know where the bad
guys were going to be.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:35.707
What is my favorite parkour
scene from film or TV?

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:37.860
That is difficult.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:40.020
I'm just going to tell you
guys to watch the entirety

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:42.720
of the "District B13" movie.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:43.700
A lot of height drops,

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:46.810
an iconic underbar,
going down the staircase.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:50.220
This was when parkour was still
more or less at its infancy.

